<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    Settings, as SettingsCard rows.

    This page was 438 lines of hand-rolled two-column grid-in-border, one copy per row, and every copy was a
    chance for a label to sit a pixel off or an AutomationProperties.Name to be forgotten. SettingsCard is the
    same layout with the keyboard and screen-reader behaviour supplied — Header, Description and a content slot,
    which is exactly the shape every row here already had.

    It is the project's first non-SDK package reference, and it was measured before being taken: a trimmed
    Release publish produces the identical 37 ILLink warnings with and without it, all of them from CsWinRT's
    ABI layer, and the published output is the same size to a tenth of a megabyte. It neither helps nor hinders
    the standing PublishTrimmed work.

    Group rhythm comes from the containers, not from margins on the headers — one StackPanel per group spacing
    its rows, an outer one spacing the groups. That is what finally let GroupHeaderStyle go: it differed from
    the shared section header only by the margin it carried.
-->
<Page
    x:Class="Ripcord_App.Pages.SettingsPage"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:local="using:Ripcord_App.Pages"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    xmlns:tk="using:CommunityToolkit.WinUI.Controls"
    Loaded="Page_Loaded"
    mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!--
        RectilinearDistance, not the default overlap rule. MEASURED, not reasoned about — three earlier
        attempts at this were guesses and all three were wrong.

        By default XY focus only considers a candidate that geometrically OVERLAPS the focused element on the
        perpendicular axis. On this page the controls do not line up: the sliders occupy x 776-996, because
        their value readout sits to their right and pushes them left, while every ToggleSwitch occupies
        x 1008-1080. A twelve-pixel gap, so a slider and a toggle have no overlap at all and are invisible to
        each other. Pressing Down on the bitrate slider stepped straight over the adaptive-bitrate toggle to
        the next ComboBox, which is wide enough to overlap.

        Projection was tried first and could not have worked: it projects the focused element's bounds along
        the direction of travel, which still requires perpendicular overlap. RectilinearDistance ranks
        candidates by distance instead and needs no overlap, which is what "the next control down" means to a
        person reading a settings list.

        On the container, not as XYFocusUp/Down element references — the plan's rule, and the reason is
        visible right here: the exact alignment that broke this is the kind of thing markup changes silently.
    -->
    <ScrollViewer Padding="24,16,24,24">
        <StackPanel
            MaxWidth="820"
            Spacing="24"
            XYFocusDownNavigationStrategy="RectilinearDistance"
            XYFocusUpNavigationStrategy="RectilinearDistance">
            <TextBlock x:Uid="SettingsPage_Settings" Style="{StaticResource TitleTextBlockStyle}" />


            <!--  ============ Picture ============  -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                <TextBlock x:Uid="SettingsPage_Picture" Style="{StaticResource RipcordSectionHeaderStyle}" />

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_PVerifiedGoodDefault">
                    <ComboBox
                        x:Name="ResolutionCombo"
                        MinWidth="180"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_ResolutionFrameRate" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <!--
                    The slider sits in the content slot with its value beside it rather than under a header,
                    because a number that moves needs to be next to the thing moving it.
                -->
                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_StartingRateRipcordLowers">
                    <StackPanel
                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                        Spacing="12"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                        <Slider
                            x:Name="BitrateSlider"
                            MinWidth="220"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            x:Uid="SettingsPage_MaximumBitrateMegabitsPer"
                            Maximum="40"
                            Minimum="2"
                            StepFrequency="1" />
                        <TextBlock
                            x:Name="BitrateValueText"
                            MinWidth="72"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            Style="{StaticResource BodyStrongTextBlockStyle}" />
                    </StackPanel>
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_LowerBitratePacketLoss">
                    <ToggleSwitch
                        x:Name="AdaptiveToggle"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_AdaptQualityAutomatically" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <tk:SettingsCard x:Uid="SettingsPage_HowStreamScaledUp">
                    <ComboBox
                        x:Name="UpscaleCombo"
                        MinWidth="180"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_ImageScaling" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <!--
                    HEVC is offered only when a decoder for it exists on this machine, so the option cannot be
                    selected into a session that then fails to start. The Description is written from the
                    view-model, because what it says depends on that answer.
                -->
                <tk:SettingsCard x:Uid="SettingsPage_VideoCodec" x:Name="CodecCard">
                    <ComboBox
                        x:Name="CodecCombo"
                        MinWidth="180"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_VideoCodec" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <!--
                    HDR expands, because the readiness checklist is the reason someone opens it. Four things are
                    needed and only one of them is this toggle, so a user whose picture stays SDR has no way to
                    tell WHICH is missing from a sentence listing them all. Each row reports its own state and,
                    when unmet, says what to do.

                    The console row is deliberately neutral rather than a tick or a cross: whether the PS5 sends
                    HDR cannot be known until a session runs, and a cross for "unknown" reads as a fault the
                    user should go and fix.
                -->
                <tk:SettingsExpander x:Uid="SettingsPage_RequestHdr">
                    <ToggleSwitch x:Uid="SettingsPage_RequestHdr2" x:Name="HdrToggle" />

                    <tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
                        <tk:SettingsCard HorizontalContentAlignment="Left" ContentAlignment="Left">
                            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
                                <StackPanel x:Name="HdrChecklist" Spacing="3" />
                                <TextBlock
                                    x:Name="HdrHelpText"
                                    Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"
                                    Style="{StaticResource CaptionTextBlockStyle}"
                                    TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                            </StackPanel>
                        </tk:SettingsCard>
                    </tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
                </tk:SettingsExpander>
            </StackPanel>

            <!--  ============ Controls ============  -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                <TextBlock x:Uid="SettingsPage_Controls" Style="{StaticResource RipcordSectionHeaderStyle}" />

                <!--
                    Keyboard input is off by default: with a pad connected there is no reason for stray typing to
                    reach the game, and the default layout overlaps ordinary window shortcuts.
                -->
                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_OffByDefaultPad">
                    <ToggleSwitch
                        x:Name="KeyboardToggle"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_SendKeyboardInputConsole" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <tk:SettingsCard x:Uid="SettingsPage_KeysGamepadMapping" x:Name="KeyBindingsCard">
                    <Button
                        x:Name="KeyBindingsButton"
                        Click="KeyBindings_Click"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_Configure" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <tk:SettingsCard x:Uid="SettingsPage_LeaveStreamController" x:Name="ExitGestureCard">
                    <ComboBox
                        x:Name="ExitGestureCombo"
                        MinWidth="180"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_LeaveStreamController" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_HowFarMustPush">
                    <StackPanel
                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                        Spacing="12"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                        <Slider
                            x:Name="DeadzoneSlider"
                            MinWidth="220"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            x:Uid="SettingsPage_MenuStickSensitivity"
                            Maximum="0.9"
                            Minimum="0.1"
                            StepFrequency="0.05" />
                        <TextBlock
                            x:Name="DeadzoneValueText"
                            MinWidth="72"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            Style="{StaticResource BodyStrongTextBlockStyle}" />
                    </StackPanel>
                </tk:SettingsCard>
            </StackPanel>

            <!--  ============ When you play ============  -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                <TextBlock x:Uid="SettingsPage_WhenYouPlay" Style="{StaticResource RipcordSectionHeaderStyle}" />

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_HidesTitleBarNavigation">
                    <ToggleSwitch
                        x:Name="FullScreenToggle"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_FullScreenWhenStream" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_ConfirmsWhenEndStream">
                    <ToggleSwitch
                        x:Name="ConfirmOnDisconnectToggle"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_AskBeforeDisconnecting" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_DefaultAnswerRestMode">
                    <ToggleSwitch
                        x:Name="RestOnDisconnectToggle"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_RestConsoleWhenI" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_FrameRateLatencyPacket">
                    <!--
                        A picker, not a switch: there are three answers now. It is also the only control a
                        pad-only player has over the HUD, since nothing in the stream layer takes gamepad
                        input by design, so "which rung" has to be answerable before connecting.
                    -->
                    <ComboBox
                        x:Name="DiagnosticsPicker"
                        MinWidth="180"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_ShowConnectionDiagnostics" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>
            </StackPanel>

            <!--
                ============ Account ============

                NOT first, though it was, and the argument for putting it there was a real one: it is the
                only section that changes what the others can do - signed in, pairing stops asking for an
                account ID and a console off this network can still be woken.

                That is about capability, not about use. LAN play against a paired console needs none of it,
                the README says so outright, and a settings page that opens on a sign-in prompt makes an
                optional account read as a prerequisite. Ordering follows what people change; a
                cross-cutting enabler does not earn the top by being cross-cutting.
            -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                <TextBlock x:Uid="SettingsPage_Account" Style="{StaticResource RipcordSectionHeaderStyle}" />

                <tk:SettingsCard x:Uid="SettingsPage_PlaystationNetwork" x:Name="AccountCard">
                    <tk:SettingsCard.HeaderIcon>
                        <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE77B;" />
                    </tk:SettingsCard.HeaderIcon>

                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                        <ProgressRing x:Name="AccountBusy" Width="20" Height="20" IsActive="False" />
                        <Button x:Uid="SettingsPage_SignIn" x:Name="SignInButton" />
                        <Button x:Uid="SettingsPage_SignOut" x:Name="SignOutButton" Visibility="Collapsed" />
                    </StackPanel>
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <!--  Only shown when there is something to say — an error, or the account's console list.  -->
                <InfoBar
                    x:Name="AccountError"
                    IsOpen="False"
                    IsClosable="False"
                    Severity="Warning" />

                <tk:SettingsExpander
                    x:Name="CloudConsolesExpander"
                    Visibility="Collapsed"
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_ConsolesAccount">
                    <tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
                        <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE7F4;" />
                    </tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
                </tk:SettingsExpander>
            </StackPanel>

            <!--  ============ Advanced ============  -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                <TextBlock x:Uid="SettingsPage_Advanced" Style="{StaticResource RipcordSectionHeaderStyle}" />

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_AutomaticPicksGpuDriving">
                    <ComboBox
                        x:Name="GpuCombo"
                        MinWidth="180"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_WhichGpuUse" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <!--  Shown only for the "specific GPU" preference — see SettingsViewModel.AdapterPickerVisible.  -->
                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Name="AdapterCard"
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_SpecificAdapter"
                    Visibility="Collapsed">
                    <ComboBox
                        x:Name="AdapterCombo"
                        MinWidth="280"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_SpecificGraphicsAdapter" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <InfoBar
                    x:Name="AdapterWarning"
                    IsClosable="False"
                    IsOpen="False"
                    Severity="Warning" />

                <tk:SettingsCard
                    x:Uid="SettingsPage_SendsMeasuredDelayPacket">
                    <ToggleSwitch
                        x:Name="ConnectionQualityToggle"
                        x:Uid="SettingsPage_TellConsoleAboutConnection" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <!--
                    Moved here from a section of its own. It is one always-open InfoBar reporting whether
                    pairing credentials are encrypted at rest - a fact about how the app stores things, which
                    is what this section is for, and not something a heading of its own made easier to find.
                -->
                <InfoBar
                    x:Name="CredentialProtectionBar"
                    IsClosable="False"
                    IsOpen="True"
                    Severity="Informational" />
            </StackPanel>
        </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>
</Page>
